 "title": "Ski jumping: small hill K 10",
 "category": "track-and-jumps",
 "time_limit_s": 20.0,
 "stages": [
  "take-off",
  "landing",
  "fall line"
 ],
 "rulebook": "FIS International Competition Rules (ICR), Book III, Ski Jumping, edition 2024: Art. 411 (jumping hills to the FIS standards), 432 (the distance: where both feet are in full contact with the landing surface; in a fall, where a body part first touches it), the fall line (the end of the landing hill's transition, where the outrun starts) and a fall before it; FIS Jumping Hills Construction Norm 2018 (implementing Art. 411): table angle alpha = w/30 + 7.4 deg, h/n between w/800 + 0.4 and w/1000 + 0.48, in-run angle gamma at most 32 deg on a beginners' hill (w < 30 m), HS = w / 0.9, beta_P = beta + alpha/2 - 2.5 deg, beta_0 = beta_P / 6, at most 0.7 g of centrifugal load at the end of the in-run's transition and 0.8 g in the landing's, distances w = 1.005 x the straight distance from the edge",
 "deviations": [
  "Art. 411 and the Construction Norm 2018 (a hill certificate from FIS's JUMP program): the hill is built from the norm's published formulas for w = 10.0 m (K 10, HS 11) with h/n = 0.451, alpha = 7.73 deg, gamma = 30.0 deg; what JUMP computes from data it does not publish is our estimate: the take-off speed 10.52 m/s that carries a point mass with the robot's drag and a take-off push of 1.0 m/s across the table to K, beta = 30.7 deg from an equivalent landing height of 0.3 m at K, the table's height 0.3 m, the knoll a cubic from beta_0 to beta_P, the landing circle's P 2.0 m before K, the transitions as circles (17.0 m in the in-run, the norm's 0.7 g at 10.52 m/s; 26.0 m to the outrun, its 0.8 g), the table 2.6 m long (about a quarter second at take-off speed).",
  "In-run length: the start is where the robot, standing still, reaches about 10.52 m/s at the edge in a crouch (12.3 m of straight in-run above the transition); there is one start (no start gates, no gate changes, no wind), and no in-run track: the skis run on the groomed in-run, held straight by the snow model's edge grip.",
  "The start: no start bench and no push-off; the robot stands still on the in-run, perpendicular to it, and is released at t = 0.",
  "Equipment: jumping skis at the robot's size (see the module's constants); the binding holds the foot rigidly (a jumping binding lets the heel rise; the robot's ankle joint takes that role) and never releases; no ski boots (the feet are bound directly); rigid skis.",
  "Air and snow: still air at 1500 m (1.052 kg/m^3) with drag on every link but no lift (the ported snow-and-air model has no lift: a V-style flight gains nothing aerodynamically); the ski-snow contact is the freeski model of the CHIGUI0/roboolympics project (MIT, ported): kinetic friction after Wolfsperger et al. 2021, the edge's hold after Mossner et al. 2013, a hard prepared snow track.",
  "Judging: no style judges; the success line is the hill's construction point (a jump that reaches K, the distance a hill is built for) with a valid landing, judged by geometry at every 1 ms physics step: the landing is the first instant both skis touch the landing hill, its distance 1.005 x the straight distance from the edge to the profile point under the middle of the feet (the norm's approximation of Art. 432), in half metres; a fall (a body part other than the feet on the snow, or the pelvis less than 0.35 m above the snow after the landing) before the fall line voids the jump; a telemark is not required."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Jump the K 10 hill: ski down the in-run, take off from the table, land on your skis at or beyond the K-point (10.0 m) and ski through the transition past the fall line without falling.",
  "rules": "FIS ski jumping, one jump on a small hill (K 10.0 m) built to the FIS Construction Norm 2018. Your feet are bound to jumping skis. You stand still at the top of the in-run, which falls at 30.0 degrees and bends (radius 17.0 m) into the take-off table (2.6 m long, falling at 7.73 degrees); the table ends at its edge (x = 0) 0.3 m above the start of the landing hill. You are released at t = 0. The take-off: both skis leave the in-run with your feet past the edge. The landing: the first moment both skis touch the landing hill; the jump's distance is 1.005 times the straight distance from the edge to the point of the hill's profile under the middle of your feet, in half metres (rounded down). The landing hill: the knoll, the landing area from P to L (the K-point at 10.0 m, the hill size HS at 10.0 m / 0.9), the transition (radius 26.0 m) to the flat outrun; the fall line is where the transition ends and the outrun starts. You succeed when, after a landing at a distance of at least 10.0 m, both feet pass the fall line without a fall, within 20 s. You fail: on a fall (a part of you other than your feet on the snow at any time, or your pelvis less than 0.35 m above the snow, measured along the hill's normal, outside the flight, or after the landing your torso tilted more than 80.0 degrees from the hill's normal; a body part that touches the hill before both skis do is a fall, and the distance is measured there), if the flight lasted less than 0.1 s, if the distance is short of 10.0 m when your feet reach the fall line, if you stop (your pelvis slower than 0.1 m/s for 1.5 s), or at 20 s.",
  "venue": "You stand at the start gate near the top of the in-run, on your skis (1.70 m jumping skis), facing down the in-run (+x), perpendicular to it; the take-off's edge is at x = 0, about 20 m ahead of you along the in-run and about 8 m lower. The in-run (30.0 degrees, 3.0 m wide) bends into the table; the landing hill (12.0 m wide) starts 0.3 m below the edge, with its P (blue), K (red) and L (green) marks across it and the fall line (yellow) where the transition meets the outrun (40.0 m long).",
  "difficulty": "The robot reaches about 10 m/s on the in-run and must jump at the right instant at the edge: too late and it rotates forward, too early and it loses speed; with no lift from the air, only the push at the edge carries it to K. In the air it must set its skis and body for a landing on a slope of about 31 degrees at 13 m/s and hold its balance through the transition's g-load."
 },
